Iteration 3 finished the remaining eight checklist items, so all 13 are done. A stalled build loop (one that keeps iterating without changing code and hits the same error every time) now gets the label `flatline` from start to finish, instead of being reported as context exhaustion.

**What changed:**
- **Progress file and summary:** `progress.md` gains two lines, `Exit class: …` and `Flatline streak: N/T`. The end-of-loop summary shows the same information.
- **Pipeline build stage:** when the loop ends with failing tests and the exit class is `flatline`, it writes `flatline` to `failure-reason.txt` and emits a `pipeline.flatline` event. Every other ending still writes `context_exhaustion`.
- **Daemon:** `classify_failure` now returns `flatline` when `progress.md` says so. A flatline gets one retry by default (`MAX_RETRIES_FLATLINE`) and goes straight to the `full` template without the extra restarts that context exhaustion gets, since fresh sessions would only repeat the failure. A loop that simply ran out of iterations still counts as context exhaustion, so it keeps the extra restarts.
- **Restarts:** each restart event now records its reason, and the per-session streak resets. If the previous session flatlined, the new session's prompt includes a "change your approach" section.
- **Root-cause analysis:** there is a new `flatline` category with its own suggested fix, checked before the context-exhaustion check.
- **Event schema:** entries added for `loop.flatline`, `loop.iteration_classified` and `pipeline.flatline`; the sync check reports no drift.
- **Docs:** `CLAUDE.md` explains the behaviour and the new `loop.flatline_threshold` setting (default 3), and the two stale auto-generated sections were refreshed.

**Tests:** a full `npm test` run passed all 163 suites. That run started before the restart changes, so I reran the suites they touch afterwards; all passed: the loop suite (78), pipeline (58), pipeline stages (30), flatline (43), daemon failure (39), root cause (34), session restart (15) and docs (18). Last iteration's failures (memory promotion, durable) did not come back.

**Something odd about the commits:** my `sw-loop.sh` edits from this iteration were already in a commit (`b7d776f2`) made by another process while I was working. That commit's message only describes part of what's in it. The content is correct, so I left it alone and mentioned it in my own commit message.

The audit note from the previous iteration was an error in the audit tool's schema, not a problem in the code, so there was nothing to fix there.

LOOP_COMPLETE
